WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.195 --> 00:00:01.135
(airhorn blowing)

00:00:01.135 --> 00:00:02.877
- I didn't know it was
gonna be a big deal.

00:00:02.877 --> 00:00:05.222
I was just trying to
make something sound cool.

00:00:05.222 --> 00:00:07.079
You know, it’s like when artists talk about

00:00:07.079 --> 00:00:09.146
oh, they made like a song in 20 minutes

00:00:09.146 --> 00:00:10.551
and they didn't know and then it becomes

00:00:10.551 --> 00:00:12.324
like the biggest hit of their career.

00:00:12.324 --> 00:00:13.192
I hope the air horn's not the biggest

00:00:13.192 --> 00:00:15.061
hit of my career but so far,

00:00:15.061 --> 00:00:16.744
it's like top three.

00:00:16.744 --> 00:00:19.577
(air horn blowing)

00:00:21.179 --> 00:00:22.201
My name is Cipha Sounds,

00:00:22.201 --> 00:00:23.850
hip-hop deejay from New York City,

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:26.372
and I popularized the air horn sound effect

00:00:26.372 --> 00:00:28.729
into a global phenomenon.

00:00:28.729 --> 00:00:33.509
(air horn blowing)
(hip-hop music)

00:00:33.509 --> 00:00:34.589
In Jamaica,

00:00:34.589 --> 00:00:37.630
in the late ’70's, early ’80's,

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:39.500
they used to have these
parties in these clubs,

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.241
which they called dance halls.

00:00:41.241 --> 00:00:43.923
And in that moment, everyone gets excited.

00:00:43.923 --> 00:00:45.827
They scream, they make noises,

00:00:45.827 --> 00:00:47.298
and they would hit air horns.

00:00:47.298 --> 00:00:48.377
And then it got to a point

00:00:48.377 --> 00:00:50.525
where deejays started
having little samplers

00:00:50.525 --> 00:00:52.905
and have it playing it themselves.

00:00:52.905 --> 00:00:55.204
Dance hall and hip-hop are

00:00:55.204 --> 00:00:57.352
basically, like, half-brothers.

00:00:57.352 --> 00:00:59.778
Deejays, especially Kool
Herc, who was Jamaican,

00:00:59.778 --> 00:01:02.890
moved to the Bronx and
would throw these parties

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:05.653
like he used to throw in Jamaica.

00:01:05.653 --> 00:01:07.322
That whole scene moved here

00:01:07.322 --> 00:01:09.133
and that's what created hip-hop.

00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:10.364
Ladies and gentlemen, it is three o'clock

00:01:10.364 --> 00:01:12.802
in New York City, yours
truly Cipha Sounds,

00:01:12.802 --> 00:01:14.323
deejaying others back.

00:01:14.323 --> 00:01:17.782
So early 2000's, around 2001, 2002,

00:01:17.782 --> 00:01:21.242
I got my own show on
Hot 97, Cipha Saturdays,

00:01:21.242 --> 00:01:22.438
and I wanted to sound different

00:01:22.438 --> 00:01:24.040
than all the other deejays.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.759
So I would use the air horn

00:01:25.759 --> 00:01:28.266
and a couple other Jamaican sound effects.

00:01:28.266 --> 00:01:30.879
But see it was hard, it was hard to play

00:01:30.879 --> 00:01:32.399
sound effects like that back in the day

00:01:32.399 --> 00:01:33.851
‘cause when you did it live in the club,

00:01:33.851 --> 00:01:34.942
you would hit the air horn

00:01:34.942 --> 00:01:36.629
Berr-berr-berr-berrrr

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:38.613
and making different, I guess, like,

00:01:38.613 --> 00:01:42.015
rhythmic variations of how it would play.

00:01:42.015 --> 00:01:43.455
So we couldn't do that in the computer

00:01:43.455 --> 00:01:44.441
so I had to, like,

00:01:44.441 --> 00:01:46.287
make my own version.

00:01:46.287 --> 00:01:48.145
So I would just hit the button once,

00:01:48.145 --> 00:01:49.910
(rhythmic air horn blowing)

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:51.233
but it would play like it was live,

00:01:51.233 --> 00:01:53.695
which is the one that kind of, like, exploded.

00:01:53.695 --> 00:01:56.434
I didn't invent the air horn sound.

00:01:56.434 --> 00:01:59.848
I didn't invent the
usage of it in a party.

00:01:59.848 --> 00:02:01.032
Me being on the radio,

00:02:01.032 --> 00:02:03.702
I just became the guy known for it.

00:02:03.702 --> 00:02:06.535
(air horn blowing)

